  title={A Multiband 4th-Order RF Transmitter Featuring an N-Path-Filter Modulator With a Switched gm--C Network}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={61},
  number={7},
  pages={3223-3232},
  abstract={This article presents a multiband radio frequency (RF) transmitter incorporating a switched transconductance ( $g_{\mathrm {m}}$ )–C network within its N-path-filter modulator to accomplish the signal bandwidth (BW) extension. With the subtraction technique between two N-path networks, each utilizing poly-phase gm cells to up-shift and down-shift the baseband (BB) admittance, an LO-defined 4th-order high-Q bandpass response is built inside the modulator. This innovation effectively suppresses the out-of-band (OB) noise and spectral replicas, without compromising the signal BW. Also, in contrast to the conventional transmitters that rely on the high-order BB low-pass filters to suppress the OB noise of the digital-to-analog converter (DAC), our transmitter additionally rejects the noises due to the BB circuitry, mixer, and LO generator. The power amplifier (PA) driver employs a Class-AB transconductor to enhance the transmitter efficiency, and a parallelized triode-region-biased linearizer reduces the output nonlinearities. Prototyped in a 65-nm CMOS process, our transmitter operates over an RF range of 1.4–2.7 GHz. When injecting a 40-MHz modulated signal, the adjacent channel leakage ratio (ACLR)1 is  $\lt -41.6$  dBc for a 2.9-dBm output power. The transmitter exhibits 3.1% power efficiency and −53.8-dBc CIM3. The OB noise floor is  $\le -156.3$  dBc/Hz. The core area is 0.29 mm2.},
  keywords={Feeds;Antennas;Band-pass filters;Capacitors;Circuits;Filtering;Filters;Low-pass filters;Phase noise;Integrated circuits;Bandwidth (BW) extended;baseband (BB);high-Q bandpass response;linearity;multiband;noise;N-path-filter modulator;out-of-band (OB);radio frequency (RF);subtraction;switched gm–C;transmitter},
