  abstract={This article presents a 3-D resonant current-mode wireless power receiver (RCM 3D-Rx) with autonomous optimum resonance cycle ( $N_{\mathrm {OPT}}$ ) detection and real-time link adaptivity for implantable medical devices (IMDs). The proposed design integrates three orthogonally oriented receiver coils within a com pact 3-D-printed PA12 structure, enabling orientation-insensitive power transfer without external programming or lookup tables (LUTs). Each receiver axis independently determines and periodically re-evaluates its  $N_{\mathrm {OPT}}$  to adapt to real-time coupling variations, while a non-residual  $V_{\mathrm {Rx}}$  feedback loop (NRFL) ensures complete energy transfer from the  $LC$  tank, improving output power by up to 38.0%. Fabricated in a 180-nm CMOS process and operating at 6.78 MHz, the prototype occupies 4.52 mm2 and supports a wide input power range from  $293~{\mu }$ W to 23.7 mW, achieving a peak power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 75.1% at 8.559 mW. Measurement results demonstrate robust wireless charging through 50 mm of tissue and reliable performance under severe spatial misalignment and dynamic link variations, validating the proposed RCM 3D-Rx as an efficient and implant-ready power solution.},
