  volume={61},
  number={7},
  pages={3460-3473},
  abstract={This work introduces a constant bandwidth oversampling PLL (CBW-OSPLL) that eliminates the noise degradation and stability limitations of conventional oversampling phase-locked loops (PLLs) caused by sampling gain fluctuations. The proposed CBW-OSPLL utilizes a constant-gain oversampler composed of double-edge capacitive charging and discharging PIs (DE-CCDPIs) and sampling single-edge capacitive charging and discharging PIs (SSE-CCDPIs) with embedded reference sampling functionality. The DE-CCDPIs provide eight clocks with phases uniformly distributed within a reference cycle, which are further evenly split into 16 phases through SSE-CCDPIs. The intrinsic sampling functionality embedded in the proposed SSE-CCDPI ensures a constant sampling across all sampling locations, enabling improved noise and stability. To further improve the linearity of the DE-CCDPIs and SSE-CCDPIs, direct-gating transition and voltage clamping techniques are proposed, collectively reducing the differential nonlinearity of the DE-CCDPIs and SSE-CCDPIs to 0.001 LSB. Fabricated in a standard 0.18- $\mu $ m CMOS, the proposed integer-N CBW-OSPLL occupies an active area of 0.44 mm2. When operating at 2 GHz, it achieves an rms jitter of 444 fs and consumes 7.6 mW, yielding an FoMJIT of −238.2 dB and an FoMREF of −240.2 dB, while measurements across supply variation, temperature variation, and multiple samples are performed, demonstrating the robustness of the proposed CBW-OSPLL.},
  keywords={Phase locked loops;Bandwidth;Noise;Gain;IP networks;Clocks;Phase noise;Circuit stability;Transient analysis;Transconductance;Area-efficient;constant bandwidth;jitter;oversampling;phase noise;phase-locked loop (PLL);ring oscillator (RO)},
