  abstract={This article presents a power-efficient, voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO)-based wideband continuous-time multistage noise shaping (MASH) 3-1 delta–sigma modulator, designed for emerging high-data-rate wireless applications. To address the challenges related to power consumption and nonlinearity under wideband low-oversampling ratio (OSR) conditions, an all-pass filtering Gm- $C$  integrator is proposed. This architecture incorporates an all-pass filter (APF) into an input-shunt feedback front-end integrator in Gm- $C$  delta–sigma modulator for path delay alignment and digital-to-analog converter (DAC) filtering and restores the noise transfer function (NTF) through coefficients recalculating. This technique effectively attenuates the residual voltage fluctuations at the pseudo-virtual ground node and mitigates signal leakage in the loop filter, relaxing the linearity requirements and the power for the Gm- $C$  integrators. Furthermore, the matching requirements of the feedback DAC can be significantly relaxed by leveraging the inherent dynamic element matching (DEM) capability of the VCO quantizer, allowing a DAC calibration-free design. The VCO quantizer-induced harmonic distortion is suppressed by nonlinearity cancellation. Implemented in a 16-nm FinFET technology, the prototype analog-to-digital converter (ADC) with all-pass filtering Gm- $C$  integrator attains a peak spurious-free dynamic range (SFDR) of 85.5 dB and an signal-to-noise-and-distortion ratio (SNDR) of 65.5 dB with a dynamic range (DR) of 68.5 dB over a 200-MHz bandwidth at a 4-GS/s sampling rate. It consumes 33 mW of power with a core area of 0.08 mm2, achieving a Schreier figure-of-merit (FoM)SNDR of 163.3 dB.},
